    {
      "id": "TMS-01",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "A covariance depends on the outside odd part, up to a constant sign",
      "anchor": "inputs-twist-pairs",
      "statement": "For q in the progression with q>nn′, the product (n/q)(n′/q) equals σ(q/s) for a sign σ independent of q, where s is the product of primes p∤m of odd valuation in nn′. Thus the square expansion can be bounded using the absolute value of ∑q(q/s).",
      "hypotheses": "The positive modulus is divisible by 8, a is coprime to it, each q is prime in that residue class, 1≤n,n′≤N and q>N². The same κ with |κ(n)|≤1 is used throughout P.",
      "definitions": [
        "Λ is the von Mangoldt function, c_n=Λ(n)min(1,y/n).",
        "s_p is the sum of all positive weights with base prime p. R sums the weights of prime powers with even exponent or whose base divides m."
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-twist-reciprocity"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "correspondence": "Quadratic reciprocity fixes the reciprocity and modulus signs when q stays in the residue class. The unclipped complex coefficient κ(n)conj(κ(n′)) is independent of q, so the absolute value may be taken after the prime average. The sign example checks that an unreduced nonsquare product can give a constant −1.",
