    {
      "id": "TMS-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Only one squarefree modulus can retain a close aligned zero",
      "anchor": "inputs-twist-exception",
      "statement": "For ε,A,B>0 and all sufficiently large Y depending on them, every admitted U,m,a has one n★ such that every odd squarefree s>1 coprime to m, s≤Y^A, s≠n★, satisfies |∑_{q∈P}(q/s)|≤Y^(−B)|P|. The caller uses A=800K and B=K+1.",
      "hypotheses": "The admitted progression has 8|m, m>0, gcd(a,m)=1, (log Y)²log m/4≤log U and Y^(ε/6)≤log U≤Y^(ε/5)(log Y)². The written proof invokes the relative prime-number theorem in progressions, the Dirichlet zero-free theorem and the exceptional-zero bound supplying the lower population estimate.",
      "definitions": [
        "An exceptional pair χ,β at modulus ms consists of a nonprincipal quadratic character and its real L-zero near 1.",
        "Alignment means χ(u)=(u/s) for every u≡1 mod m coprime to s. The cutoff for the close zero uses D=mY^A, common to all s.",
        "The denominator s★ indexes a quadratic symbol in a covariance. It is selected for the whole progression before κ and y."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "dependencies": [],
      "correspondence": "The uniform li(U)/φ(ms) part of the progression main term sums to zero against the nonprincipal Jacobi character. For misalignment, multiplication by a unit of the fiber makes the exceptional sum its own negative. For aligned cases outside the single exception β≤1−c₀/log D gives a sufficiently small li(U^β). Page uniqueness at the product modulus and the separation of distinct squarefree Jacobi symbols show that at most one s can be close and aligned. twistK sets n★=0 if none exists. The progression floor and the relative PNT error absorb the √U lower-cutoff term.",
