    {
      "id": "TMS-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The two pair costs and the absolute mean-square constant",
      "anchor": "inputs-twist-budget",
      "statement": "The covariance sum is bounded by |P|[∑s_p²+R²+2B²+2BR+ηT²]. With Z=(Y^K)^400, w=Y^(ε/5), η=Y^(−K−1), it is therefore ≤Cy log(y)|P| for Y≤y≤Y^K. A single C works before ε,K are chosen.",
      "hypotheses": "0<ε≤1, K≥1, eventual Y after fixing ε,K, U,m,a admitted. Every prime dividing m is ≤w or >Z, N=⌊Z⌋, and κ is any one common bounded sequence. A coefficient can depend on the common y in each application.",
      "definitions": [
        "T=∑c_n, B=C₂ log y≥s_p, R sums even powers or modulus bases, s_p sums all powers with that base."
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-twist-assembly"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "TMS-01",
        "TMS-02"
      ],
      "correspondence": "pairs_one pays Σs_p² for identical bases and R² for different bases with both residual parts empty. For exceptional r prime pairs_r_prime pays s_r²+2s_rR; for a composite r the complementary factors and the two orders cost at most 2B². Other pairs cost ηT². Positive arithmetic estimates give R≤5C₁√y, Σs_p²≤C₃y log y, and T≤C₄y log Z. The eventual bounds (log Z)²≤Y and y≤Y^K give ηT²≤C₄²y. The written constant is C₃+25C₁²+4C₂²+20C₁C₂+C₄². StagePow applies the resulting common-height bound before the derivative/variable-height argument.",
